* MVPKG.FILE - read and write whole OS files.
* Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.  GPL-2.0-only (see LICENSE).
*
*   FOP    in   "READ" | "WRITE" | "DELETE"
*   FPATH  in   the OS path
*   FDATA  in/out  the contents, lines separated by CHAR(10)
*   FOK    out  1 on success, 0 on failure
*
* READ RETURNS THE FILE'S BYTES, NEWLINES AND ALL.  This header used to say @AM
* and no caller believed it: every one of them parses with CHAR(10) --
* STOREHEAL's operator marker, MVPKG.FIXPERMS' "am I mvpkg" check,
* CALLC.EXISTS' function list, MVPKG.REG's registry URL.  Only two of the four
* ports actually produced @AM, so the contract was three different things at
* once and the callers were right about one of them.
*
* MEASURED, on the same two-line file:
*
*   MVX        OSREAD -> raw bytes         DCOUNT nl=3 am=1
*   UniData    OSREAD -> raw bytes         DCOUNT nl=3 am=1
*   UniVerse   READSEQ, joined here        DCOUNT nl=1 am=2   <- was wrong
*   jBASE      OSREAD -> @AM per line      DCOUNT nl=1 am=2   <- was wrong
*
* So UniVerse joins with CHAR(10) and jBASE converts back; MVX and UniData
* already did the right thing.  What this cost: STOREHEAL read the store
* owner's path as the empty string on both ports, wrote a VOC file pointer to
* "/MVPKG.STORE", and `MVPKG list` could not open the store (#118).
*
* THE TWO PORTS THAT REWRITE ALSO LOSE THE TRAILING NEWLINE, so a file ending
* "\n" reads back one byte shorter there than on MVX/UniData.  Every caller
* TRIMs or FIELDs, so nothing depends on it -- but it is not byte-exact and
* should not be relied on to be.
*
* WRITE IS DELIBERATELY LENIENT and takes either separator: UniVerse splits on
* @AM and writes what is left as one line -- so CHAR(10)-separated data goes
* out verbatim, correctly -- and jBASE's OSWRITE turns @AM back into newlines.
* Callers pass both (INCSETUP builds PLATFORM.H with @AM, STOREHEAL writes the
* marker with CHAR(10)) and both land as proper text files.
*
* MVX, UniData and jBASE have OSREAD / OSWRITE.  UNIVERSE HAS NEITHER, and
* again says so unhelpfully -- it reads `OSREAD X FROM P` as a variable being
* assigned:
*
*     Variable Name (UNDEFINED) unexpected, Was expecting: Assignment Operator
*
* There the equivalent is OPENSEQ / READSEQ / WRITESEQ, with the wrinkle that
* OPENSEQ on a path that does not exist yet FAILS, and CREATE is what makes it.
*
* Note OSWRITE takes no ELSE clause on UniData or jBASE -- writing
* `OSWRITE X ON F ELSE ...` is a syntax error on both.
   SUBROUTINE MVPKG.FILE(FOP, FPATH, FDATA, FOK)
$INCLUDE MVPKG.INC PLATFORM.H
   FOK = 0
   FOP = OCONV(FOP, "MCU")
   IF TRIM(FPATH) = "" THEN RETURN
*
$IFDEF UV
   BEGIN CASE
      CASE FOP = "READ"
* JOINED WITH CHAR(10), NOT @AM -- READ RETURNS THE FILE'S BYTES.  See the note
* at the head of this file: every caller parses with CHAR(10).
*
* FFIRST, not `IF FDATA = ""`: a file whose first line is blank left FDATA empty,
* so the SECOND line became the first and every line number after it was off by
* one.  The flag says "nothing read yet", which is the actual question.
         FDATA = ""
         OPENSEQ FPATH TO FFV THEN
            FDONE = 0 ; FFIRST = 1
            LOOP UNTIL FDONE DO
               READSEQ FLINE FROM FFV THEN
                  IF FFIRST THEN
                     FDATA = FLINE ; FFIRST = 0
                  END ELSE
                     FDATA := CHAR(10) : FLINE
                  END
               END ELSE FDONE = 1
            REPEAT
            CLOSESEQ FFV
            FOK = 1
         END
      CASE FOP = "WRITE"
         OPENSEQ FPATH TO FFV ELSE
            CREATE FFV ELSE RETURN
         END
* An existing file is truncated first, or a shorter write leaves the tail of
* the previous one behind and the file reads as a mixture of both.
         WEOFSEQ FFV
         FN = DCOUNT(FDATA, @AM)
         FOR FI = 1 TO FN
            WRITESEQ FDATA<FI> ON FFV ELSE NULL
         NEXT FI
         CLOSESEQ FFV
         FOK = 1
      CASE FOP = "DELETE"
         CALL MVPKG.SH('rm -f "' : FPATH : '"', FO, FS)
         FOK = 1
   END CASE
$ENDIF
*
$IFDEF UDT
* An OSREAD/OSWRITE body cannot be shared through a GOSUB: the label's contents
* sit outside every guard, so UniVerse still compiles them and still cannot
* parse OSREAD.  Each platform that has the statements carries its own copy.
   BEGIN CASE
      CASE FOP = "READ"
         OSREAD FDATA FROM FPATH THEN FOK = 1 ELSE FDATA = ""
      CASE FOP = "WRITE"
* OSWRITE takes no ELSE clause on either system -- that is a syntax error.
         OSWRITE FDATA ON FPATH
         FOK = 1
      CASE FOP = "DELETE"
         CALL MVPKG.SH('rm -f "' : FPATH : '"', FO, FS)
         FOK = 1
   END CASE
$ENDIF
$IFDEF JBASE
* An OSREAD/OSWRITE body cannot be shared through a GOSUB: the label's contents
* sit outside every guard, so UniVerse still compiles them and still cannot
* parse OSREAD.  Each platform that has the statements carries its own copy.
   BEGIN CASE
      CASE FOP = "READ"
* jBASE's OSREAD IS NOT UniData's.  UniData (and MVX) hand back the file's bytes
* with its newlines; jBASE turns every newline into an @AM on the way in and
* drops the trailing one.  Convert back, so READ means the same thing here.
*
* The CONVERT *statement* is used deliberately: the CONVERT() *function* takes
* its arguments in the opposite order on jBASE and returns rubbish rather than
* failing (mv_package#54).  The statement is correct here and MVX -- which
* rejects it -- never compiles this arm.
         OSREAD FDATA FROM FPATH THEN FOK = 1 ELSE FDATA = ""
         CONVERT @AM TO CHAR(10) IN FDATA
      CASE FOP = "WRITE"
* OSWRITE takes no ELSE clause on either system -- that is a syntax error.
         OSWRITE FDATA ON FPATH
         FOK = 1
      CASE FOP = "DELETE"
         CALL MVPKG.SH('rm -f "' : FPATH : '"', FO, FS)
         FOK = 1
   END CASE
$ENDIF
$IFDEF MVX
   BEGIN CASE
      CASE FOP = "READ"
         FDATA = OSREAD(FPATH)
         IF STATUS() = 0 THEN FOK = 1 ELSE FDATA = ""
      CASE FOP = "WRITE"
         IF OSWRITE(FDATA, FPATH) THEN FOK = 1
      CASE FOP = "DELETE"
         CALL MVPKG.SH('rm -f "' : FPATH : '"', FO, FS)
         FOK = 1
   END CASE
$ENDIF
   RETURN
